secur32: return an NTSTATUS from LsaFreeReturnBuffer, not VirtualFree's BOOL

`LsaFreeReturnBuffer` returns the result of `VirtualFree( buffer, 0, MEM_RELEASE )`, which is a BOOL:
TRUE (1) on success, FALSE (0) on failure.  Its only consumer treats the value as an NTSTATUS, so
under Wine a *successful* free is reported as 0x00000001 - `SEC_E_INVALID_HANDLE` - and a *failed*
free is reported as STATUS_SUCCESS:

    /* dlls/secur32/lsa.c:316 */
    NTSTATUS WINAPI LsaFreeReturnBuffer( PVOID buffer )
    {
        TRACE("%p\n", buffer);
        return VirtualFree( buffer, 0, MEM_RELEASE );
    }
    /* dlls/secur32/secur32.c:647 - the only consumer of the value */
    SECURITY_STATUS WINAPI FreeContextBuffer( void *pv )
    {
        if (!RtlValidateHeap( GetProcessHeap(), 0, pv )) return LsaFreeReturnBuffer( pv );
        RtlFreeHeap( GetProcessHeap(), 0, pv );
        return SEC_E_OK;
    }

This is the call Power BI's MSOLAP OLE DB provider makes immediately after it finishes its
authentication handshake, and the two platforms disagree on it - same probe, same provider, one
platform each, measured on the call itself:

    Wine    logs/msolap/sspi11.probe.out        [sspi] FreeContextBuffer(…) -> status=0x00000001
    Windows logs/msolap/winprobe_sspi_ref.txt   [sspi] FreeContextBuffer(…) -> status=0x00000000
            logs/msolap/winprobe_engine_ok.out  [sspi] FreeContextBuffer(…) -> status=0x00000000

Both platforms take the same branch: the freed pointer is the `SecPkgInfoW` that
`QueryContextAttributes( SECPKG_ATTR_PACKAGE_INFO )` hands back, which the LSA package allocates in
the caller's process with `AllocateClientBuffer`, so `RtlValidateHeap` is false and the
`VirtualFree` branch is the one that runs.  The Windows capture prints that explicitly
(`HeapValidate=not the process heap`), so the branch selection is identical and only the returned
value deviates: VirtualFree's TRUE arrives at the provider as 1.

Windows behaviour restored: a successful `FreeContextBuffer`/`LsaFreeReturnBuffer` reports success.
Nothing else changes - the failure path now returns a real error (`STATUS_INVALID_ADDRESS`) instead of
claiming success, and the process-heap branch (`RtlFreeHeap`, which already returned SEC_E_OK) is
untouched.

Verified:

  * Same-probe differential before the fix, quoted above (Wine 0x00000001 vs Windows 0x00000000), on the
    same call, same provider, one platform each.
  * Engine-free proof of the value, with the fix installed and everything else unchanged
    (`logs/msolap/freebufprobe_fixed_wine.txt`; the probe is the same shape as `tools/winapi/ntlmprobe.c` -
    a loopback `Negotiate` handshake in one process, then `QCA(SIZES)`, `QCA(PACKAGE_INFO)` and
    `FreeContextBuffer` on both the LSA-allocated buffer and a process-heap one):

        handshake round 1: client=0x00090312 out=74
        handshake done:      client=0x00000000 server=0x00000000
        QCA(SIZES)           status=0x00000000  cbMaxToken=1904 cbMaxSignature=16 cbBlockSize=0 cbSecurityTrailer=16
        QCA(PACKAGE_INFO)    status=0x00000000  name=NTLM
        FreeContextBuffer(lsa buffer) status=0x00000000  PASS  (want 0x00000000, as Windows returns)
        FreeContextBuffer(heap buffer) status=0x00000000  PASS  (the RtlFreeHeap branch)

    Before the fix the second line reported `0x00000001` (the Wine captures above); the heap branch was
    already `SEC_E_OK` and is unchanged.
  * The provider's differential and the application-level acceptance on the build containing this patch and
    `0003` are recorded in `FINDINGS.md` (M41) and `docs/MSOLAP_CONNECTION.md` (§8), including whether
    `Initialize()` flips to `S_OK`.

Scope, stated plainly: this is a separate defect found on the same path, not part of the SPNEGO
framing in `0003-secur32-negotiate-spnego.patch`; that patch does not touch this file and does not
claim to fix this.  The inverted return is a defect either way - whether the provider's
`Initialize()` turns on it is what the run above measures.
--- a/dlls/secur32/lsa.c
+++ b/dlls/secur32/lsa.c
@@ -316,7 +316,10 @@
 NTSTATUS WINAPI LsaFreeReturnBuffer(PVOID buffer)
 {
     TRACE("%p\n", buffer);
-    return VirtualFree(buffer, 0, MEM_RELEASE);
+    /* VirtualFree returns a BOOL (TRUE on success); reporting that as an NTSTATUS makes a *successful* free
+     * look like a failure to the caller (secur32.FreeContextBuffer, its only consumer of the value) and a
+     * failed one look like success.  Windows returns success here. */
+    return VirtualFree(buffer, 0, MEM_RELEASE) ? STATUS_SUCCESS : STATUS_INVALID_ADDRESS;
 }
 
 NTSTATUS WINAPI LsaGetLogonSessionData(PLUID LogonId,
